  {
    "uid": "C1130",
    "text": "Aquinas argues that fire can work well as fire by heating and burning while harming something else. This challenges the claim that every harmful effect needs a wholly evil source. It still leaves the question of why a knowing creator would make such a world.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4759,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "wonder body",
        "owner": "plain-divinity-limited-answer",
        "field": "text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1131",
    "text": "Aquinas thinks natural reasoning can establish some things about the cause of the world, but not the Trinity. He can therefore explain the belief in one God as Father, Son and Spirit without claiming that ordinary observations prove it.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4761,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "wonder body",
        "owner": "plain-divinity-reason-boundary",
        "field": "text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1132",
    "text": "Augustine says a person still chooses when helped by God. Aquinas says an axe really cuts while a craftsperson works through it. The limited comparison shows that dependent action can be real; it does not reduce people to tools.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4777,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "wonder body",
        "owner": "plain-divinity-help-not-erasure",
        "field": "text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1133",
    "text": "Luther describes repentance as a lifetime of turning away from wrong. A seller's promise that a coin guarantees release for a dead soul cannot replace that changed life or give the seller control over God's mercy. His criticism does not declare every church rite or payment fraudulent.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4787,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "wonder body",
        "owner": "plain-divinity-no-paid-shortcut",
        "field": "text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1134",
    "text": "At Marburg, the signers affirm one God as Father, Son and Spirit and receiving Christ through faith. They still disagree about his bodily presence in bread and wine. A shared signature records real agreement without erasing its limit.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4801,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "wonder body",
        "owner": "plain-divinity-bounded-agreement",
        "field": "text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1135",
    "text": "Luther and the Council of Trent both deny, in this household comparison, that feeding someone buys the initial gift of being made right with God. This limited agreement does not make their accounts of faith, inward renewal or later good deeds the same.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4805,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "wonder body",
        "owner": "plain-divinity-gift-common-ground",
        "field": "text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1136",
    "text": "A new record may help establish whose bone the fragment is. Calvin would still reject giving it religious honour even if its identity were proved, while Trent allows honour to genuine relics. Better identity evidence does not settle that religious disagreement.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4821,
